WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

NOTE Screen 4 of WB-M01 — audio/wb-m01-s4.mp3

s4-1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.894
There are two reasons to work this way, and the first one is simply that it is more accurate.

s4-2
00:00:06.644 --> 00:00:15.546
Think about what happens when a recurring problem gets treated as a people problem. The person is coached, or moved, or replaced.

s4-3
00:00:15.966 --> 00:00:24.170
And then the next person arrives — conscientious, well-intentioned, new — and inherits the same layout, the same forms,

s4-4
00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:30.984
the same two systems that don't talk to each other. Within a few weeks, the same thing goes wrong again.

s4-5
00:00:31.734 --> 00:00:46.000
Deming demonstrated this with what he called the Red Bead Experiment. Volunteers scoop beads from a box with a paddle, and every scoop comes up with some red beads — because a fifth of the beads in the box are red.

s4-6
00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:58.906
He praised the workers who drew fewer red beads, warned the ones who drew more, and eventually fired the worst performers. Their results never changed. The beads were in the box.

s4-7
00:00:59.656 --> 00:01:04.444
That pattern is the single most reliable signal you will learn to spot.

s4-8
00:01:04.864 --> 00:01:10.409
If a problem survives a change of personnel, the problem was never in the personnel.

s4-9
00:01:10.829 --> 00:01:14.978
It is built into the work, waiting for whoever shows up next.
